turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**Why:** Under IRC §852(b)(6), a RIC recognizes no gain when it distributes appreciated securities to meet redemptions. ETF redemptions run through authorized participants, so the fund can deliver its lowest-basis lots, often using \"heartbeat\" trades. This purges embedded gains without realizing them. A cash-dealing mutual fund must sell stock to pay redeeming holders. Those realized net gains must be distributed, as RICs effectively distribute nearly all income to avoid fund-level and excise tax. The remaining holders then owe tax on gains they did not choose to realize.\n\n**Limits:**\n- The benefit is mostly deferral. You pay capital-gains tax when you sell your ETF shares.\n- Dividends remain taxable each year.\n- Gains can still arise from cash redemptions, cash mergers, forced rebalancing, or index changes.\n- Tax-deferred accounts gain nothing from this.\n- Results depend on flows and turnover.\n- Legislative risk remains: Congress has floated repealing §852(b)(6) for ETFs.\n- Step-up at death can eliminate deferred gain, but that is estate-dependent.","ttft_ms":6047,"type":"result","duration_ms":10039,"uuid":"c6c8ceef-0ba3-4905-82c7-9e2e6e1957c7","ttft_stream_ms":1235,"time_to_request_ms":54,"first_content_frame_ms":1235,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
